1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,663
Lines, angles, and triangles questions
give you a figure and a few facts.

2
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:07,484
The whole skill is knowing
which angles are equal,

3
00:00:07,504 --> 00:00:11,304
which ones add up to one eighty, and
which triangles are secretly the same

4
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:12,026
shape.

5
00:00:12,046 --> 00:00:16,276
The mistake that costs the most points
is using a fact the question never gave

6
00:00:16,296 --> 00:00:16,955
you.

7
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:19,867
Treating lines as parallel
because they look parallel.

8
00:00:19,975 --> 00:00:22,347
Matching the wrong sides of two triangles.

9
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:25,598
We'll fix both, then work
through six practice questions,

10
00:00:25,618 --> 00:00:26,929
ending with a hard one.

11
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:29,643
Two lines that cross make four angles.

12
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:32,500
The angles across from each
other are vertical angles,

13
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,285
and they're always equal.

14
00:00:34,305 --> 00:00:37,249
Angles next to each
other form a linear pair.

15
00:00:37,269 --> 00:00:40,786
Together they make a straight
line, so they add up to one eighty.

16
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:46,179
So if one angle is seventy degrees, the
angle across from it is also seventy,

17
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:48,913
and the other two are
each one hundred ten.

18
00:00:49,055 --> 00:00:51,485
One number gives you all four.

19
00:00:51,541 --> 00:00:54,128
Now two parallel lines
cut by a third line,

20
00:00:54,148 --> 00:00:55,613
called a transversal.

21
00:00:55,684 --> 00:00:58,613
That makes eight angles,
but only two sizes.

22
00:00:58,755 --> 00:01:01,986
The small angles are all equal,
the big angles are all equal,

23
00:01:02,006 --> 00:01:04,791
and a small one plus a
big one is one eighty.

24
00:01:04,854 --> 00:01:08,048
The SAT uses the official
names, so know them.

25
00:01:08,068 --> 00:01:12,047
Corresponding angles sit in the
same position at each crossing,

26
00:01:12,067 --> 00:01:13,334
and they're equal.

27
00:01:13,354 --> 00:01:16,119
Alternate interior angles
are between the lines,

28
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:18,120
on opposite sides of the transversal,

29
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:19,571
and they're equal too.

30
00:01:19,591 --> 00:01:21,750
Same side interior angles
are between the lines,

31
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:23,821
on the same side of the transversal.

32
00:01:23,841 --> 00:01:25,250
Those are not equal.

33
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:27,213
They add up to one eighty.

34
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:31,876
Mixing up equal and supplementary is
one of the most common angle mistakes.

35
00:01:31,937 --> 00:01:34,560
All of this only works if
the lines are parallel,

36
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:37,846
and the question has to say
so or mark them with arrows.

37
00:01:37,866 --> 00:01:42,095
The test directions say figures are
drawn to scale unless noted otherwise,

38
00:01:42,115 --> 00:01:44,310
so a figure can help you check an answer.

39
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:46,937
But looking parallel
doesn't prove anything.

40
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:47,940
Triangles.

41
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:50,905
The three angles of a
triangle add up to one eighty.

42
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:52,440
That one is on the reference sheet.

43
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:54,118
The next rule isn't.

44
00:01:54,138 --> 00:01:56,976
An exterior angle, made
by extending one side,

45
00:01:56,996 --> 00:02:01,039
equals the sum of the two interior
angles that aren't next to it.

46
00:02:01,059 --> 00:02:03,610
If those two angles are
forty and seventy-five,

47
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:06,718
the exterior angle is one fifteen.

48
00:02:06,738 --> 00:02:10,345
Check it: the third interior
angle is sixty-five,

49
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:14,059
and one eighty minus
sixty-five is one fifteen.

50
00:02:14,083 --> 00:02:16,348
Isosceles triangles have two equal sides,

51
00:02:16,368 --> 00:02:19,118
and the angles opposite
those sides are equal.

52
00:02:19,225 --> 00:02:21,741
Equilateral triangles
have three equal sides,

53
00:02:21,761 --> 00:02:24,047
so every angle is sixty degrees.

54
00:02:24,076 --> 00:02:26,090
Similar triangles have the same shape,

55
00:02:26,110 --> 00:02:28,270
but not always the same size.

56
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:31,162
Their angles are equal, and
their sides are proportional.

57
00:02:31,182 --> 00:02:33,794
Two pairs of equal angles
is enough to prove it.

58
00:02:33,814 --> 00:02:35,206
Order matters.

59
00:02:35,492 --> 00:02:40,044
If triangle A B C is
similar to triangle D E F,

60
00:02:40,064 --> 00:02:44,721
then A matches D, B
matches E, and C matches F.

61
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:49,044
So A B over D E equals B C over E F.

62
00:02:49,064 --> 00:02:51,528
Write the match before
you write a proportion.

63
00:02:51,566 --> 00:02:56,617
The SAT's favorite setup is a segment
drawn parallel to one side of a triangle.

64
00:02:56,637 --> 00:03:00,046
It cuts off a small triangle
that's similar to the big one,

65
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:03,617
because the parallel lines make
equal corresponding angles.

66
00:03:03,637 --> 00:03:06,709
Compare the small triangle
to the whole big triangle.

67
00:03:06,738 --> 00:03:10,004
Congruent triangles have the
same shape and the same size.

68
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:14,647
Side side side, side angle
side, angle side angle,

69
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:18,075
and angle angle side all prove congruence.

70
00:03:18,095 --> 00:03:21,042
Three angles alone only prove similarity.

71
00:03:21,062 --> 00:03:23,418
Here's how to spot what a question wants.

72
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:25,613
Parallel means angle pairs.

73
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:28,577
Equal sides or tick marks mean isosceles.

74
00:03:28,597 --> 00:03:33,193
A side extended to a new point means
an exterior angle or a linear pair.

75
00:03:33,213 --> 00:03:37,586
A segment parallel to a side, or
two triangles with matching angles,

76
00:03:37,606 --> 00:03:40,300
means similar triangles and a proportion.

77
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:44,713
And the word sufficient means the
congruence and similarity rules.

78
00:03:44,755 --> 00:03:45,755
Question one.

79
00:03:45,827 --> 00:03:49,128
In the figure, line L
is parallel to line M,

80
00:03:49,148 --> 00:03:51,414
and line T crosses both.

81
00:03:51,434 --> 00:03:56,627
Where T crosses line L, the angle above
L and to the left of T measures two x

82
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:58,127
plus fifteen degrees.

83
00:03:58,147 --> 00:04:03,914
Where T crosses line M, the angle above
M and to the right of T measures four x

84
00:04:03,934 --> 00:04:05,521
minus fifteen degrees.

85
00:04:05,541 --> 00:04:07,469
What is the value of x?

86
00:04:07,567 --> 00:04:08,781
Pause and try it.

87
00:04:12,994 --> 00:04:15,008
These two angles aren't
a standard pair yet,

88
00:04:15,028 --> 00:04:16,366
so move one.

89
00:04:16,386 --> 00:04:20,366
The angle across from two x plus
fifteen is its vertical angle,

90
00:04:20,386 --> 00:04:22,671
below line L and right of T.

91
00:04:22,994 --> 00:04:24,035
Same measure.

92
00:04:24,055 --> 00:04:28,821
Now that angle and four x minus
fifteen are both between the lines,

93
00:04:28,841 --> 00:04:30,321
on the same side of T.

94
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:33,428
Same side interior angles
add up to one eighty.

95
00:04:33,448 --> 00:04:37,034
So six x equals one
eighty, and x is thirty.

96
00:04:37,054 --> 00:04:38,642
The answer is B.

97
00:04:38,662 --> 00:04:42,749
Check it: the angles are
seventy-five and one hundred five,

98
00:04:42,769 --> 00:04:45,590
a small one and a big
one, like the figure.

99
00:04:45,768 --> 00:04:48,125
A comes from setting them equal.

100
00:04:48,269 --> 00:04:51,233
C and D are the angles, not x.

101
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:52,382
Question two.

102
00:04:52,846 --> 00:04:57,005
In triangle A B C, A B equals A C.

103
00:04:57,025 --> 00:05:00,775
Side B C is extended past C to point D.

104
00:05:00,918 --> 00:05:05,417
The measure of angle A C D is
one hundred fifteen degrees.

105
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:09,790
What is the measure, in
degrees, of angle B A C?

106
00:05:09,810 --> 00:05:11,024
Pause and try it.

107
00:05:14,237 --> 00:05:19,038
Angle A C D and angle A
C B form a linear pair,

108
00:05:19,058 --> 00:05:22,717
so angle A C B is one
eighty minus one fifteen,

109
00:05:22,737 --> 00:05:24,165
which is sixty-five.

110
00:05:24,275 --> 00:05:28,719
A B equals A C, so the equal angles
are the ones opposite those sides:

111
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:30,469
angle C and angle B.

112
00:05:30,489 --> 00:05:32,861
So angle B is also sixty-five.

113
00:05:32,881 --> 00:05:35,791
That leaves one eighty minus
one thirty for angle A.

114
00:05:35,811 --> 00:05:36,877
Enter fifty.

115
00:05:36,897 --> 00:05:41,841
Exterior angle check: fifty
plus sixty-five is one fifteen.

116
00:05:41,861 --> 00:05:45,483
The trap is treating angle A
as one of the equal angles.

117
00:05:45,503 --> 00:05:48,932
The equal angles always sit
opposite the equal sides.

118
00:05:48,973 --> 00:05:50,096
Question three.

119
00:05:50,116 --> 00:05:56,238
In triangle P Q R, point S lies on
side P Q and point T lies on side P R,

120
00:05:56,258 --> 00:06:00,739
so that segment S T is
parallel to side Q R.

121
00:06:00,759 --> 00:06:05,025
If P S is six, S Q is
nine, and P T is eight,

122
00:06:05,045 --> 00:06:06,830
what is the length of T R?

123
00:06:06,864 --> 00:06:08,078
Pause and try it.

124
00:06:12,291 --> 00:06:18,735
S T is parallel to Q R, so triangle
P S T is similar to triangle P Q R.

125
00:06:18,755 --> 00:06:21,627
Compare small to big, using whole sides.

126
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:24,377
P Q is six plus nine, or fifteen.

127
00:06:24,397 --> 00:06:28,398
So six over fifteen equals eight over P R.

128
00:06:28,433 --> 00:06:33,091
Cross multiply: six times
P R equals one twenty,

129
00:06:33,111 --> 00:06:35,040
so P R is twenty.

130
00:06:35,325 --> 00:06:38,789
But the question asks for
T R, the piece below T.

131
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:41,290
Twenty minus eight is twelve.

132
00:06:41,503 --> 00:06:43,026
The answer is C.

133
00:06:43,046 --> 00:06:47,312
Shortcut: a parallel segment cuts
both sides in the same ratio,

134
00:06:47,332 --> 00:06:50,991
so six over nine equals eight over T R.

135
00:06:51,011 --> 00:06:53,275
That works for the
pieces of the cut sides,

136
00:06:53,295 --> 00:06:56,511
but for S T and Q R, use whole sides.

137
00:06:56,546 --> 00:06:58,989
D is P R, the whole side.

138
00:06:59,009 --> 00:07:00,526
A flips the ratio.

139
00:07:00,546 --> 00:07:03,668
B adds three, because nine
is three more than six.

140
00:07:03,688 --> 00:07:06,276
Similar triangles scale by multiplying,

141
00:07:06,296 --> 00:07:07,608
never by adding.

142
00:07:07,628 --> 00:07:08,822
Question four.

143
00:07:08,842 --> 00:07:15,822
In triangles J K L and R S T, angles J
and R each measure thirty-five degrees,

144
00:07:15,842 --> 00:07:19,786
and angles K and S each
measure eighty degrees.

145
00:07:19,806 --> 00:07:23,608
Which additional piece of information
is sufficient to determine whether

146
00:07:23,628 --> 00:07:29,128
triangle J K L is congruent
to triangle R S T?

147
00:07:29,150 --> 00:07:30,365
Pause and try it.

148
00:07:34,577 --> 00:07:37,592
Two pairs of equal angles
make the triangles similar.

149
00:07:37,612 --> 00:07:38,700
Same shape.

150
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:43,343
Congruent also needs the same size, so
you need to compare one pair of matching

151
00:07:43,363 --> 00:07:44,272
sides.

152
00:07:44,292 --> 00:07:51,006
J matches R and K matches S,
so side J K matches side R S.

153
00:07:51,049 --> 00:07:55,993
If J K equals R S, the triangles
are congruent by angle side angle.

154
00:07:56,013 --> 00:07:57,636
If not, they're different sizes.

155
00:07:57,656 --> 00:07:59,100
Either way, you can decide.

156
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,534
The answer is A.

157
00:08:00,554 --> 00:08:04,748
B adds nothing: the third angles
are already sixty-five degrees each.

158
00:08:04,768 --> 00:08:08,247
C is one length from one triangle,
so there's nothing to compare.

159
00:08:08,267 --> 00:08:09,747
D is the trap.

160
00:08:09,767 --> 00:08:12,661
Equal angles prove similar, not congruent.

161
00:08:12,689 --> 00:08:15,490
Question five, where
the traps come together.

162
00:08:15,510 --> 00:08:20,010
In triangle A B C, point
D lies on side A B,

163
00:08:20,082 --> 00:08:22,939
and point E lies on side A C.

164
00:08:23,653 --> 00:08:27,832
Angle A D E is congruent to angle A C B.

165
00:08:28,225 --> 00:08:32,776
If A B is twelve, A C
is nine, and A D is six,

166
00:08:32,796 --> 00:08:34,832
what is the length of A E?

167
00:08:34,885 --> 00:08:36,099
Pause and try it.

168
00:08:40,312 --> 00:08:43,719
It's tempting to treat
D E as parallel to B C,

169
00:08:43,739 --> 00:08:45,685
but the question never says that.

170
00:08:45,705 --> 00:08:48,578
What it gives you is one
pair of equal angles:

171
00:08:48,598 --> 00:08:52,205
angle A D E and angle A C B.

172
00:08:52,312 --> 00:08:55,042
Both triangles also share angle A.

173
00:08:55,062 --> 00:08:57,407
Two pairs of equal angles,
so they're similar.

174
00:08:57,427 --> 00:08:59,086
Now match carefully.

175
00:08:59,106 --> 00:09:00,550
A matches A.

176
00:09:00,570 --> 00:09:03,300
D matches C, because those
are the equal angles.

177
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:05,514
That leaves E matching B.

178
00:09:05,534 --> 00:09:09,943
So triangle A D E is
similar to triangle A C B,

179
00:09:09,963 --> 00:09:10,989
in that order.

180
00:09:11,009 --> 00:09:13,008
Read the sides from that order.

181
00:09:13,115 --> 00:09:17,330
A D over A C equals A E over A B.

182
00:09:17,438 --> 00:09:20,809
Six over nine equals A E over twelve.

183
00:09:20,829 --> 00:09:22,525
Two thirds of twelve is eight.

184
00:09:22,545 --> 00:09:24,320
The answer is C.

185
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:30,820
B is what you get if you assume D E is
parallel to B C and match A D with A B.

186
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:34,212
D flips the ratio, and it's
longer than all of A C,

187
00:09:34,232 --> 00:09:35,213
which is impossible.

188
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:37,396
A subtracts instead of scaling.

189
00:09:37,416 --> 00:09:39,038
Those are the three big traps.

190
00:09:39,058 --> 00:09:41,987
Assuming lines are parallel
because they look that way.

191
00:09:42,058 --> 00:09:45,324
Matching the wrong sides, so
write the vertex match first.

192
00:09:45,344 --> 00:09:48,806
And mixing up equal and
supplementary angles.

193
00:09:48,826 --> 00:09:50,912
For that last one, use the picture.

194
00:09:50,932 --> 00:09:55,842
Small equals small, big equals big,
and a small one plus a big one is one

195
00:09:55,862 --> 00:09:56,510
eighty.

196
00:09:56,530 --> 00:09:58,474
Question six, the hardest one.

197
00:09:58,494 --> 00:10:01,260
Line L is parallel to line M.

198
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:05,724
Points Q and A lie on line
L, with Q to the left of A.

199
00:10:05,744 --> 00:10:10,510
Points B and R lie on line
M, with R to the right of B.

200
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:12,887
Point P lies between the two lines.

201
00:10:12,992 --> 00:10:17,472
The measure of angle Q A P is
one hundred forty-two degrees,

202
00:10:17,492 --> 00:10:21,419
and the measure of angle R
B P is forty-seven degrees.

203
00:10:21,526 --> 00:10:25,527
What is the measure, in
degrees, of angle A P B?

204
00:10:25,592 --> 00:10:26,500
Pause and try it.

205
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:28,020
Take a little longer on this one.

206
00:10:32,234 --> 00:10:33,929
First, the linear pair at A.

207
00:10:33,949 --> 00:10:39,428
Angle Q A P is one forty-two, so
the angle between A P and line L,

208
00:10:39,448 --> 00:10:42,535
on the right side of A, is one
eighty minus one forty-two.

209
00:10:42,555 --> 00:10:44,376
That's thirty-eight degrees.

210
00:10:44,509 --> 00:10:47,597
Now the trick for any bent
path between parallel lines.

211
00:10:47,617 --> 00:10:50,561
Draw a new line through
P, parallel to both.

212
00:10:50,581 --> 00:10:54,044
It splits angle A P B into two pieces.

213
00:10:54,170 --> 00:10:58,757
The top piece and the thirty-eight degree
angle are alternate interior angles,

214
00:10:58,777 --> 00:11:00,364
so the top piece is thirty-eight.

215
00:11:00,384 --> 00:11:05,043
The bottom piece and the forty-seven
degree angle are alternate interior angles

216
00:11:05,063 --> 00:11:07,419
too, so the bottom piece is forty-seven.

217
00:11:07,533 --> 00:11:10,370
Add them: thirty-eight plus
forty-seven is eighty-five.

218
00:11:10,390 --> 00:11:12,120
Enter eighty-five.

219
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:14,798
If you added one
forty-two and forty-seven,

220
00:11:14,818 --> 00:11:16,726
you used the wrong angle at A.

221
00:11:16,746 --> 00:11:19,104
That's why you fix the linear pair first.

222
00:11:19,135 --> 00:11:21,472
Here's the method for any lines, angles,

223
00:11:21,492 --> 00:11:23,135
or triangles question.

224
00:11:23,171 --> 00:11:25,564
One: mark only what's given.

225
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:28,400
Parallel arrows, tick marks, right angles.

226
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:30,008
Two: angle chase.

227
00:11:30,028 --> 00:11:32,722
Fill in every angle you
can with linear pairs,

228
00:11:32,742 --> 00:11:34,992
vertical angles, and the one eighty rule.

229
00:11:35,097 --> 00:11:38,382
Three: for lengths,
find similar triangles.

230
00:11:38,489 --> 00:11:41,077
Write the vertex match,
then the proportion,

231
00:11:41,097 --> 00:11:42,668
using whole sides.

232
00:11:42,847 --> 00:11:44,597
Four: stuck?

233
00:11:44,704 --> 00:11:47,719
Add a line, like a parallel
line through a point.

234
00:11:47,739 --> 00:11:50,470
Five: reread what they ask for.

235
00:11:50,490 --> 00:11:52,327
The angle or x?

236
00:11:52,347 --> 00:11:54,298
The whole side or a piece?

237
00:11:54,318 --> 00:11:58,191
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

238
00:11:58,211 --> 00:12:02,997
lines, angles, and triangles
under Geometry and Trigonometry.

239
00:12:03,104 --> 00:12:06,798
Every question in this video
was written for this video,

240
00:12:06,818 --> 00:12:09,176
in the style of the real test.

241
00:12:09,208 --> 00:12:12,045
Next up: right triangles and trigonometry.

242
00:12:12,065 --> 00:12:14,831
The Pythagorean theorem,
special right triangles,

243
00:12:14,851 --> 00:12:17,315
and sine, cosine, and tangent.

244
00:12:17,423 --> 00:12:19,636
If this helped, subscribe so you catch it.
